  {
    "title": "Gaydos: House Democrats Put Politics Ahead of Public Safety",
    "url": "http://RepGaydos.com/News/38380/Press-Releases/Gaydos--House-Democrats-Put-Politics-Ahead-of-Public-Safety",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Gaydos: House Democrats Put Politics Ahead of Public Safety\nJuly 24, 2026\nHARRISBURG\n- Rep. Valerie Gaydos (R-Allegheny) voted against\nHouse Bill 1042\nafter House Democrats amended the legislation, replacing a bipartisan Senate-passed solution with provisions that weaken accountability for violent offenders and create uncertainty for crime victims and their families.\n“The Pennsylvania Supreme Court gave the General Assembly 120 days to address this issue. On day 119, I voted in favor of returning to the Senate’s bipartisan bill because it could have been on the governor’s desk today, providing a clear and timely response to the court’s ruling,” said Gaydos. “Instead, House Democrats abandoned that bipartisan approach at the last minute, replacing it with their own proposal that favors convicted killers over victims and public safety. Pennsylvanians deserve better.”\nThe Senate-passed bill established sentencing guidelines for individuals convicted of second-degree murder following the Pennsylvania Supreme Court's ruling. Rather than sending that bipartisan measure to the governor, House Democrats amended the bill to add medical parole provisions and replace the Senate's sentencing framework with language that gives judges broad discretion in sentencing individuals convicted of second-degree murder.\n“With the deadline Friday, July 24, and no remedy in place, the more than 1,100 prisoners convicted of murder who are affected by this lapse could challenge their sentences, potentially opening the door to their release into Pennsylvania communities,” said Gaydos.\n“Pennsylvania families deserve a criminal justice system that prioritizes victims, protects our communities and holds violent offenders accountable. Instead of advancing a bill that had already passed the Senate, House Democrats loaded it with unrelated provisions that make an already difficult issue even more complicated.\n“Legislation affecting public safety should never be rushed through the House with major amendments released at the last minute. Pennsylvanians deserve an open process and laws that put public safety first, not politics.”\nGaydos voted “yes” on the motion to restore the Senate language and “no” on the amended House bill.\nHouse Bill 1042 passed the House and now moves to the Senate on concurrence.\nRepresentative Valerie Gaydos\n44th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Rick Leiner\n717.260.6437\nrleiner@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Valerie Gaydos voted against House Bill 1042 after House Democrats amended it to add medical parole provisions and give judges broader sentencing discretion for second-degree murder convictions. She had previously voted in favor of a motion to restore the original Senate version of the bill. The amended bill passed the House and now goes to the Senate for approval.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Gaydos Invites Residents, Parents to Join the Fight Against Human Trafficking",
    "url": "http://RepGaydos.com/News/38397/Press-Releases/Gaydos-Invites-Residents--Parents-to-Join-the-Fight-Against-Human-Trafficking",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Gaydos Invites Residents, Parents to Join the Fight Against Human Trafficking\nJuly 30, 2026\nHARRISBURG\n- Rep. Valerie Gaydos (R-Allegheny), a member of the Pennsylvania Anti-Human Trafficking (AHT) Caucus, is inviting residents to attend a free community event to learn how to recognize the warning signs of human trafficking and help protect vulnerable people, especially children.\nThe event will be held Tuesday, Sept. 1, at 6 p.m., at the Sewickley Hills Borough Building,\n349 Magee Road\n, Sewickley. Pennsylvania Attorney General Dave Sunday will be the guest speaker.\n“Human trafficking happens in communities across Pennsylvania, and many victims are hiding in plain sight,” said Gaydos. “Parents need to know that traffickers are increasingly targeting children through social media, online gaming, false promises of friendship or employment, and by exploiting family or financial hardships. Knowing the warning signs can make all the difference. The more our families and communities understand how traffickers operate, the better equipped we are to protect our children, stop this crime and support victims.”\nHotels, motels and short-term rentals are among the most common places where human trafficking, especially sex trafficking, occurs. Transportation hubs, including airports, also play a role in moving victims, making public awareness and training especially important. Victims may be guests, but they can also be employees working in housekeeping, cleaning services or other support roles. Traffickers also increasingly target children online through social media, gaming platforms and other digital spaces, often using manipulation, false promises and deception to gain their trust before exploiting them.\nThis year, Gaydos voted for two new laws aimed at cracking down on human trafficking.\n•\nAct 31 of 2026\nrequires human trafficking awareness training for employees in Pennsylvania's hospitality industry so they can recognize suspicious activity and know how to respond.\n•\nAct 41 of 2026\ngives the Pennsylvania Attorney General and law enforcement stronger tools to investigate and prosecute traffickers while increasing penalties for those who exploit vulnerable victims.\n“These new laws are an important step forward, but laws alone will not end human trafficking,” said Gaydos. “Protecting our children starts with awareness. When parents, grandparents, teachers, neighbors and community members know what to look for, they’re better prepared to recognize the warning signs and intervene before it’s too late. That’s why I hope families throughout our community will join us for this important discussion.”\nThe free event will help attendees:\n• Understand how traffickers target and exploit children.\n• Learn the warning signs of human trafficking.\n• Discover practical ways to help protect their families.\n• Learn how to report concerns and support victims.\nAnyone who suspects human trafficking should contact the National Human Trafficking Hotline at 1-888-373-7888 or text 233733 (BEFREE). In an emergency, call 911.\nRepresentative Valerie Gaydos\n44th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Rick Leiner\n717.260.6437\nrleiner@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Valerie Gaydos is hosting a free public event on September 1, 2026 in Sewickley focused on human trafficking awareness, featuring Pennsylvania Attorney General Dave Sunday as a speaker. She also voted in favor of two recently enacted state laws requiring human trafficking awareness training for hospitality industry employees and strengthening law enforcement tools to prosecute traffickers.",
      "action_type": "event",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "education"
      ],
      "spin_flag": false
    }
  },
