  {
    "title": "Public Hearing on Accountability, Justice in Second-Degree Murder Sentencing",
    "url": "http://RepGaydos.com/News/38469/Press-Releases/Public-Hearing-on-Accountability--Justice-in-Second-Degree-Murder-Sentencing",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Public Hearing on Accountability, Justice in Second-Degree Murder Sentencing\nAugust 13, 2026\nWHAT:\nHouse Republican Policy Committee Chairman David H. Rowe (R-Snyder/Union/Mifflin/Juniata), Rep. Valerie Gaydos (R-Moon Township), Rep. Natalie Mihalek (R-Allegheny/Washington), Rep. Jeremy Shaffer (R-Allegheny), and other lawmakers will host a hearing titled “Protecting Pennsylvania: Accountability and Justice in Second-Degree Murder Sentencing” to examine how Pennsylvania can establish a statutory second-degree murder sentencing framework that holds offenders accountable, protects the public, and gives victims’ families a meaningful voice.\nWHO:\nTestifiers will include Stella Getgen, wife of Todd Getgen (written testimony to be read aloud); Christine Hickman, sister of Todd Getgen (written testimony to be read aloud); Ronald M. Wabby, Jr., deputy district attorney, Critical Case Review Unit, Appellate Division; Nicole Ziccarelli, district attorney, Westmoreland County; and Robert Manzi Jr., district attorney, Indiana County.\nWHEN:\nThursday, Aug. 13, at 1 p.m.\nWHERE:\nThe Township of Moon Municipal Building,\n1000 Beaver Grade Road, Moon Township, PA 15108\nLIVESTREAM:\nBarring any technical difficulties, the hearing will be streamed online at\npolicycommittee.com\n.\nMedia contact:\nNancy Nilson,\nnnilson@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Republican members of the Pennsylvania House Policy Committee held a public hearing to examine how Pennsylvania should structure sentencing guidelines for second-degree murder convictions. The hearing featured testimony from victims' family members and prosecutors, focusing on offender accountability and victims' rights. No legislation was passed; this was an information-gathering event.",
      "action_type": "event",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": false
    }
